ity, Tokyo, Japan<br />He continued his study at Graduate School of Environmental Science.<br />Katsuyoshi Tomono<br />For his research on thermal waste recovery system from industrial cooling water, he was conferred a master degree of environmental science from Hokkaido University in 1984.<br />Senior Engineering Advisor, Tokyo Engineering Consultants, Co., Ltd., Japan<br />Then, he worked for a construction company<br />Keywords<br />Obayashi Corporation, from 1984 to 1997 as a mechanical engineer on building service works.<br />acute effects and chronic effect, adequate sanitation, appropriate technology, aquaculture, community participation, composting toilet, faeco-oral transmission, fish cultivation, johkasou, open defecation, pit latrine, population growth, primary health ca<br />In the meantime, he was dispatched to Thailand from 1990 to 1995.<br />Contents<br />He was dedicated to many construction and renovation projects (mainly hi-tech factories).<br />Introduction<br />His responsibilities covered air-conditioning, plumbing, water and wastewater treatment, utility supply, and pollution control.<br />Constrains to improving water and sanitation services<br />From 1997, he enrolled to Japan Overseas Cooperation Volunteers (JOCV), in the frameworks of Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA).<br />Health implications of some major water development projects<br />He spent two years in Morocco as an urbanist of the municipality of Fes.<br />Reclaimed water and health issues<br />He was assigned to "Service Nettoiement et Parc Municipal" with responsibility for research and planning of a solid waste management system.<br />Aquaculture water reuse and health<br />In 1999, he returned to Japan and resumed his study in Hokkaido University as a PhD student at Environmental Risk Engineering Laboratory, School of Engineering.<br />Related Chapters<br />He obtained a PhD in 2003 for his dissertation entitled "Appropriate technology of domestic wastewater management for low-income urban communities."<br />Glossary<br />Sombo T. Yamamura HTM1 is visiting fellow of the Institute of Advanced Studies at The United Nations University, where he has been on faculty since 2001.<br />Bibliography<br />He was admitted to Kyoto University and received the degree of Bachelor of Engineering in Sanitary Engineering in 1974.<br />Biographical Sketches<br />Then, he continued his study at the Department of Urban Engineering, The University of Tokyo and was conferred Master of Engineering in 1976.<br />Aquaculture water reuse and health<br />Immediately after graduation, he commenced his professional career at Department of Water Supply, Ministry of Health and Welfare, Japan.<br />Fish and other marine products which are captured either from fresh water or seawater are essential protein sources for human being.<br />After working for the Ministry for ten years, he was dispatched to the Department of Public Works in Indonesia as an advisor of JICA (Japan International Cooperation Agency) in 1986.<br />Although there are differences between countries, people who live in an area located at easy access to aquatic environments consume more fish than people without this facility.<br />In 1988, he moved back to Japan and served as an Engineer at Office of Environmental Impact Assessment, Environment Agency.<br />Fish generally have a life cycle of egg, larva, adult fish and spawning, and they have a life style specific to each stage.<br />;;Since 1992, he developed his carrier at several national and international organizations.Since 1992, he developed his career at several national and international organizations.<br />Therefore, the impact of water pollution on an aquatic organism largely depends on the stage in the life cycle, as well as the type of organism.<br />He served as a chief at Office of Drinking Water Quality Management, Ministry of Health and Welfare, Japan from 1992 to 1994, as a special advisor for Environmental Health Department at Environment Agency, Japan from 1994 to 1996, a JICA chief advisor for Environment Management Center in Indonesia from 1996 to 1998, then as a sanitary engineer of Water Sanitation and Health Unit, Department of Protection of the Human Environment, Cluster of Sustainable Development and Healthy Environments at WHO (World Health Organization).<br />Each species and individual in an aquatic environment is not living independently, but has close relations with other species and individuals - the whole comprising an interacting ecosystem.<br />In the meantime, he also obtained a Diploma at Imperial College, University of London in 1983.<br />Fish cultivation including artificial hatching needs a great amount of water, and water reuse is a necessary measures for economic reasons and conservation of limited water resources.<br />Katsuyoshi Tomono HTM1 is Senior Engineering Adviser of Environmental Planning Institute, Tokyo Engineering Consultants, Co., Ltd., where he has worked since 1999.<br />It is vital, however, to pay attention to water quality, not only for preventing adverse effects on the cultured fish and the ecosystem, but also for protection of the health of the people using cultivated fish as a protein source.<br />He graduated from Hokkaido University and received the degree of Bachelor of Engineering in Sanitary Engineering in 1961.<br />Effect on physiological function of aquatic life<br />After graduation, he worked for Nihon Suido Consultants, Co., Ltd. from 1961 to 1980.<br />The toxic effects of water pollution are classified into acute effects and long-term chronic effects.<br />He served as Manager of Design Division with responsibilities for planning, design and construction supervision of water supply and sewer system projects in Japan and abroad, and studies on water treatment engineering and economic evaluation on projects.<br />One of the obvious features of water pollution is the death of aquatic life, but many effects are longer-term-deterioration or abnormalities of physiological functions, the development of which can be either acute or chronic.<br />He then spent seven years as Project Engineer at Infrastructure Department, Asian Development Bank until 1987.<br />Death of aquatic organisms is mainly caused by poisoning, asphyxiation due to oxygen deficit, destruction of epidermal tissue affected by corrosive poison such as acids and alkalis, and so on.<br />He was responsible for appraisal and evaluation of bank-financed loan projects in the water supply, sewerage and sanitation sectors.<br />;;A large number of agents including heavy metals such as copper, cadmium, zinc, led, and chromium, agricultural chemicals, and synthetic chemicals such as phenol and cyanogens, are categorized as toxic agents.A large number of agents including heavy metals such as copper, cadmium, zinc, lead, and chromium, agricultural chemicals, and synthetic chemicals such as phenol and cyanogens, are categorized as toxic agents.<br />From 1987 to 1999, he worked for Japan Water Works Association as Senior Researcher for several fields, which include development studies on advanced water treatment, high-pressure water service, risk management, etc.<br />Death from suffocation is mainly effected by decrease of dissolved oxygen, caused by excessive organic materials in water systems.<br />He has authored or co-authored many research articles at the Japan Water Works Association and American Water Works Association for more than 20 years.<br />Fish sometimes suffer from oxygen starvation because turbid components of mud, fibers in pulp mill effluent, or oil, accumulate on the surface of fish gills.<br />The subject of study includes the art of water treatment in Japan, the costs and benefits of risk management in water supply, economies of scale in water supply, etc.<br />They often die of suffocation, or their body surface is damaged by corrosive poison such as acid, alkali, organic acid, or hydrogen sulfide.<br />One of the methods of estimating the toxicity in water is bioassay that uses responses of captive animals as the judgmental standard.<br />Toxicity to fish is represented by HTM1 value (Median Lethal Concentration).<br />This generally indicates the concentration that results in the death of 50% of test aquatic organisms within a fixed time period-96 hours for fish and 24 hours for HTM1 Daphnia HTM2 were recommended in the OECD guidelines for testing chemicals.<br />In contrast, for terrestrial mammals, HTM1 (Median Lethal Dose) was used<br />the specific observation period depends on test organism.<br />The former, however, represents the toxic concentration in the water, while the latter represents the dosage to the body of the test organisms.<br />It should be noted that HTM1 value is a mere index of relative toxicity of specific materials under specific test conditions<br />the value does not indicate the concentration that is considered to be safe or harmless in the contaminated water.<br />Water quality standards should be maintained so that fish and other aquatic life can live and propagate normally, and fisheries can be managed sustainably.<br />It should be noted, however, that water for aquatic life cannot be defined in terms of concentration of a single substance.<br />The quality is affected by the different type of life, differences even within one species, between various environmental conditions, by individuality and seasons, and antagonistic or synergistic effects of coexisting elements.<br />General limiting conditions for water quality are given in Table 10.<br />Table 10.<br />Limit concentrations for fish<br />Water quality for aquatic life<br />Although there are some differences between countries, people of island countries such as the United Kingdom and Japan consume a high proportion of animal protein from fish or aquatic products.<br />Therefore, even though toxic components in the water do not directly harm the physiology and biology of the aquatic organisms, many problems exist from the viewpoint of human beings who regularly eat aquatic organisms.<br />Bioconcentrated effects on fish from polluted ambient water make the commercially valuable fishes smell.<br />Odorized fish cause huge damage to the fishing industry.<br />It is said that the concentration limits to odorize fish are 1.7 ppm for petroleum ether extracts, 0.86 ppm for creosote, 1.89 ppm for fuel oil and 0.89 ppm for waste oil.<br />It is recognized that 0.01 ppm of oil odorizes water, and the water odorizes the marine fish there within 24 hours.<br />It is known that petroleum refining waste, phenol-included waste, and pulp mill effluent also odorize aquatic organisms.<br />It is clear that the components of the odorant are not single materials, but complexes of several components such as saturated hydrocarbons, unsaturated hydrocarbons, aliphatic and aromatic hydrocarbons and thiophene compounds.<br />Aquatic organisms living in polluted water that contains pathogenic bacteria or toxic components, are a threat to the health of any human beings that may eat them.<br />More than 90% of food poisoning caused by eating aquatic products is linked to bacterial contamination.<br />For example, there have been many cases of outbreaks of typhoid as a result of consumption of polluted shellfish, particularly farmed oysters from water polluted with domestic sewage.<br />In addition, many cases have been reported of poisoning by toxic compounds within the tissues of aquatic animals.<br />Other than toxins HTM1 in vivo HTM2 such as Tetrodotoxin and Ciguatera-type toxin, the important thing in relation to water pollution is bioaccumulation of toxic components in water, and subsequent intoxication.<br />Once a pollutant enters the water system, it can be accumulated by organisms or in bottom sediments through biological or physiochemical process.<br />Bioaccumulation progresses up the food chain, say from plankton to small animals and then large animals.<br />Accumulation of a specific component in an organism is closely associated with its physiology and metabolic activity.<br />Therefore, it is often the case that a specific component is accumulated in one organism, but not in another, even in the same waters.<br />Furthermore, even within an organism, the cumulative dose generally depends on the organs and tissue.<br />Oysters, for example, tend to concentrate copper and zinc in their tissues.<br />To prevent effluent from aquaculture having serious effects on public health, the reuse of aquaculture water has become a priority.<br />Systems for reuse of aquaculture water are called closed recirculating aquaculture systems.<br />They are aimed at avoiding eutrophication and contamination of surface water and groundwater.<br />With recirculating systems, the water can be purified and completely reused, and there is no wastage because the system is equipped with a foam-separation unit (for removing suspended substances) and a denitrification unit.<br />This new system and a traditional flow-through system, in which there is a continuous supply of fresh seawater, were tested with a culture of flounder.<br />There was no difference in the growth rates between the two systems.<br />If the closed recirculating aquaculture system can be applied to all aquaculture methods, it will be able to decrease marine eutrophication and nutrient-salt contamination of resource water.<br />Aquaculture water reuse will then make a positive contribution to the maintenance of human health and convenience.<br />Related Chapters<br />Glossary<br />Appropriate technology<br />The technical solution for most low-income communities, which applies relatively simple and robust systems with low-cost in order to avoid financial deficiency.<br />Assessment 2000<br />The Global Water Supply and Sanitation Assessment 2000, a joint monitoring program by WHO and UNICEF.<br />Ciguatera<br />A form of human poisoning caused by the consumption of subtropical and