United Nations statements on race.<br />The Twentieth Century Jolt<br />A good pedagogical start.<br />The twentieth century ushered in more sobering reflections.<br />The Universal Declaration on The Human Genome and Human Rights<br />The colonial empires disintegrated and by the end of the century no particular civilization was any longer regarded as enjoying an inherent cultural monopoly on scientific and technical prowess.<br />The UNESCO Courier.<br />Technical breakthroughs continued at increasing pace, radically transforming all aspects of practical life from hygiene to communications, to the point that each successive twentieth-century generation came to perceive itself as engulfed in unprecedented, indeed revolutionary, change.<br />The uncontested merit of this text resides in the balance it strikes between safeguarding respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms and the need to ensure freedom of research.<br />Science’s hugely increased role in society’s life in turn spurred still more scientific endeavor, with success breeding further success.<br />Biographical Sketch<br />The century saw exponential growth in the number of scientists and technicians, of scientific publications and scientific meetings.<br />Koichiro Matsuura HTM1 of Japan was appointed on 12 November 1999 to serve as Director-General of UNESCO.<br />Scientific methodology and tools rapidly evolved.<br />Mr Matsuura, born in Tokyo in 1937, has served as Japan’s Ambassador to France since 1994.<br />Meanwhile the rift between pure and applied science narrowed, and in many areas the distinction became difficult to discern.<br />He was educated at the Law Faculty of the University of Tokyo and at the Faculty of Economics of Haverford College (Pennsylvania, USA) and began his diplomatic career in 1959.<br />At this turn of the century, today’s pure research may find technical application tomorrow, and become part of civil society’s daily life within two years, or even less.<br />Posts held by Mr. Matsuura include those of Director-General of the Economic Co-operation Bureau of Japan’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs (1988); Director-General of the North American Affairs Bureau, Ministry of Foreign Affairs (1990); and Deputy Minister for Foreign Affairs.<br />Scientists can no longer claim that their work will have no immediate effects on the welfare of the individual, or on the policies of the state.<br />He also served as the Chairperson of UNESCO’s World Heritage Committee for the year ending November 1999.<br />Twentieth-century change also showed at far deeper conceptual levels.<br />While exploration of the farthest recesses of the human psyche proceeded apace, basic cosmological assumptions were jolted by tremendous advances in physics particularly in the first half of the century, and in biology, during the second.<br />Evolutionism’s dogmas were questioned, and while Max Planck’s early twentieth-century work still deeply affects modern mathematics, the hypotheses of quantum mechanics were tested by the end of the century in relation to a possible veiled reality.<br />Science’s own self-image was transformed.<br />Its twentieth-century practitioners showed far less moral self-assurance than their nineteenth-century or earlier predecessors.<br />Scientists no longer pretended to hold all the answers to the world’s mysteries, and no single scholar could still claim to master every aspect of his or her own particular field, as twentieth-century research accumulated and multiplied through ever more complex ramifications.<br />French biochemist and 1965 Nobel prizewinner François Jacob pointedly wrote in this regard, that the time when circumscribed issues were substituted for general ones is when modern science began.<br />Science at the Moral Crossroads<br />One of the most wrenching upheavals in twentieth-century science was moral.<br />Humanity’s demonstrated capacity to put its new tools to the most evil use—to dominate or kill—took on appalling proportions by the first half of the century, shattering complacent assumptions that scientific, technical, and ethical "progress" went naturally hand in hand.<br />The twentieth century’s searing experiences have taught us the very worst that human beings are capable of inflicting upon one another, regardless of technical sophistication, when all ethical standards lapse.<br />Since the mid-twentieth century, humans have faced an awesome realization.<br />We have multiplied technical means not only to cure more hitherto fatal illnesses (though tragically not yet all), convey ourselves farther in space, or speed our mail, but also to destroy ourselves and the planet we occupy.<br />We live from now on in moral awareness of our own power permanently to soil, or altogether to annihilate, our environment—and to abolish not only our own species, but every other living species as well.<br />No previous group of human beings in history has ever wielded such power.<br />No traditional human civilization has ever thought it necessary to focus its main attention in the way that we must now, on issues of science and applied technology.<br />Nor were the ethical leaders of past centuries, guardians of the world’s great spiritual traditions, called upon to face quite the same the stark moral choices that we must urgently make today<br />to deal, that is, with natural surroundings no longer stable but in constant flux.<br />Hence the crucial issue before us today<br />to define the ethics of science and technology as the necessary moral guidelines of our age.<br />The Threat to our Planet<br />At the time of writing, fear of nuclear self-destruction has receded through what we hope will prove a durable thaw in international relations.<br />But this menace which darkened four previous decades has now given way to other perceived, and hardly less deadly, threats, at least over the long term<br />such as rapid depletion of irreplaceable natural resources, extinction of whole animal species, irreversible pollution and degradation of the oceans, rivers, and inland seas, of the plant cover, and of our common biosphere, and finally, global warming as a consequence of our thoughtless emission into the atmosphere of the fossil fuels that we burn daily.<br />Desiccation of whole swathes of the planet’s surface, a dangerous rise in sea levels, and a permanent shred in our protective ozone layer, were some of the ominous warnings seriously repeated by many sober, scientifically fully qualified observers of human industry’s impact on nature as the twentieth century ended.<br />We know that our globe makes up a natural unit whose precious biodiversity ignores political frontiers.<br />But will leading industrial nations show the necessary self-restraint and mature responsibility to reduce looming perils to our environment over the coming decades?<br />Transcending immediate economic interests, ecology is really a HTM1 moral HTM2 option, one of HTM3 ethically HTM4 deciding what we wish to do to our earthly home, over the long run, with our knowledge and tools.<br />Hopes raised by the UN-hosted Summit in Rio de Janeiro in 1992, then by the Kyoto Summit in 1997, were temporarily dashed when the Hague Summit in 2000 failed to yield a consensus of views among the great economic powers.<br />But other meetings will, of course, follow.<br />The search for a comprehensive solution to environmental threats will be pursued, because it must.<br />A growing number of citizens and leaders are realizing that the ethics of science is not only a moral imperative, but also one that addresses human survival.<br />The issue of global warming is truly becoming a question, not only of extra-human cosmic accident, but, to repeat, of human choice<br />hence of ethics.<br />Tinkering with the Alphabet-Blocks of Life<br />Eugenics is another case in point.<br />Breakthroughs triggered by rapidly developing disciplines like computer science, embryology, medically-aided procreation techniques, neuroscience, or robotics are opening up an entire new era of surgery.<br />Healthier babies are delivered every day thanks to our astounding new tinkering with the fundamentals of human biology.<br />We are now in a position to read the basic "alphabet-blocks" of life itself, as many scientists like to phrase it.<br />The imminent completion of human genome mapping makes hitherto undreamt-of therapies and preventions possible.<br />But is everything technically possible also morally admissible?<br />When recent experiments in cloning raised possibilities of application to humans, shock waves jolted the media and rocked public opinion.<br />Governments have generally agreed not to authorize reproductive cloning for humans.<br />But what about cloning for therapeutic purposes?<br />And will future medical or, indeed, political authorities always resist temptation to manipulate genetics in order to produce the "perfect child"?<br />Tomorrow is the proverbial looking-glass, through which we only see darkly<br />will the ruling powers of an age to come, for benevolent or malevolent reasons, wish to twist the chemical make-up of human beings into serving their own purposes?<br />The Asilomar meeting in 1975 was a milestone in the bioethics movement.<br />For the first time, scientists publicly stated their awareness of the new powers and consequences of science, particularly in the area of genetics, in view of enhancing potential benefits for the human race, but controlling potential hazards.<br />The Widening Gap Between Rich and Poor<br />Another disturbing trend, one demanding both far-reaching ethical and soundly practical answers, is today’s widening technological gap<br />between the Earth’s wealthier centers of scientific research and application, and vast poorer areas left painfully deprived of either.<br />Whole human communities would suffer inadmissible loss of control over their own fate if allowed to fall yet farther behind the more favored lands in terms of access to scientific information, to vital medical services, or to the latest agricultural techniques.<br />Biotechnology, for instance, offers revolutionary new crops that may certainly help humans overcome hunger and malnutrition forever.<br />But how do we ensure that mass production of these same crops in the industrialized lands might not, at the same time, overwhelm the struggling farming economies of the developing countries, and increase their already crushing dependence for food on the scientifically so-called "developed" world?<br />While not strictly speaking a scientific issue in itself, but a question more generally related to the popularization of scientific knowledge, today’s widening educational divide between "rich" and "poor" raises an equally serious ethical problem.<br />Before many of the world’s developing countries were even able to make good their serious deficit in books, schoolrooms, and other traditional educational facilities, the information revolution at the turn of this century multiplied the speed and spread of every kind of data through a truly planetary electronic network<br />provided users were privileged enough to purchase, or be furnished with, all the necessary machinery.<br />Computer screens deliver treasures of knowledge at a touch of the fingers, the literal meaning of "digital," thus hugely expanding the human mind’s reach and vastly extending our educational range.<br />But those who enjoy no access to such tools, which still includes whole categories of the human family, are left entirely outside today’s cultural mainstream<br />stranded, as it were, in an educational backwater.<br />The very existence of harsh new catch phrases, like the current cliché that separates today’s "info-poor" from the "info-rich" on either side of a planetary "digital gap," points to growing and widespread public awareness of a serious educational imbalance both between, and within, the world’s various national communities.<br />For the bulk of the world’s population, in effect, science is a blind sphinx, distant and omnipotent, whose mysteries concern only the rich.<br />The planet can no longer remain split between the privileged and the excluded.<br />The latter are nine-tenths of the world’s population.<br />Yet our planet’s common future is the same for all.<br />If today’s technological acceleration is suffered to widen and worsen this already deplorable extant "gap," not only between the "info-poor" and "info-rich," but between the deprived and the wealthy in absolute terms, such a chasm could prove, in a foreseeable future, even more difficult (not to say despairingly impossible) to bridge, with possibly disastrous social consequences.<br />To allow our planet permanently to remain split between the haves and have-nots would be no real "progress" at all.<br />Here again, carefully defined moral and legal codes do offer humanity at least some safeguards against unwarranted scientific—and moral—deviations.<br />How we decide to direct our technical might is indeed an issue of sheer ethics<br />to be weighed anew, in light of both abiding cultural standards, and fresh cultural challenges.<br />For all our pride in our new learning and latest equipment, we are not really so different from every past generation of our species.<br />We, too, must mentally struggle to make meaning of our natural surroundings, and to take stock of our practical behavior within such surroundings, in terms of the values that we want to cherish, moral guidelines we wish to adopt, and ethical code by which we intend to live.<br />UNESCO as the World’s Forum for Ethics<br />As the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization, UNESCO is deeply committed to defend and promote universal human dignity within a dramatically changing world, while helping to raise the great moral questions and to define the ethical guidelines so keenly required, and so urgently needed to be recognized, by all peoples and lands.<br />UNESCO remains the world’s forum for earnest debate on these issues, at a time when the brunt of funding for scientific research in the universities and other centers of learning is visibly shifting from government support to backing by private enterprise.<br />Such a shift could result in a change of focus in scientific research itself if universities are increasingly invited to look for sponsorship from industry<br />or being told, indeed, that their research must be financially self-supporting.<br />In such disciplines as molecular biology or genetic engineering, much current research is already being underwritten by private firms<br />largely the pharmaceutical industry.<br />Such private sponsorship is dynamic, creative, and very welcome.<br />But financial profit, rather than intellectual advancement, can never become the sole major motivation for research.<br />The great moral options must be pondered and the ethical guidelines drawn, in view of the well-being of all the planet’s dwellers.<br />In the year 2000 alone, UNESCO initiated or participated in key meetings on issues like care for water resources, the ethics of space policies, or bioethics and the rights of the child, attended by the most eminent scholars, in various cities of the world.<br />How do we ensure that all countries, including the poorest, receive their due share from scientific developments which so far they cannot afford?<br />How do we encourage solidarity with the developing lands on the part of the wealthier nations, and common concern for saving this planet’s remaining biodiversity, in an age when a growing number of citizens and leaders now see the ethics of science as part of a collective logic of survival?<br />Clearly the ethics of tomorrow must be taught to tomorrow’s young, but along which theoretical and practical lines, should we introduce the subject of such ethics in the world’s schools, while taking due account of the aspirations, structures and needs of different countries and cultures?<br />Preoccupation with the ethics of science can be the exclusive right of no single society, no particular mentality, since the concern is the world’s.<br />Science and technology have made all lands interdependent.<br />Joint, unselfish action is called for to enable vital ideas like these to take lasting root, as the responsibility not only of decision-makers, also of every individual citizen.<br />The International Bioethics Committee<br />In 1993, UNESCO established the International Bioethics Committee (IBC), which now includes thirty-six members from all regions of the world, who sit in their personal capacity, and represent all fields concerned by bioethics<br />among which are biology, medicine, law, philosophy, sociology, and anthropology.<br />In parallel, an Intergovernmental Bioethics Committee (IGBC), made up of representatives of thirty-six Member States elected by the General Conference of UNESCO, was inaugurated in 1998.<br />The International Bioethics Committee meets annually, and the Intergovernmental Bioethics Committee every two years.<br />When entrusted with preparing an international legal instrument on the human genome, the IBC chose a two-pronged strategy.<br />First, it favored a pragmatic approach, in light of the most recent advances in genetics and their applications.<br />In this regard, the Committee examined the ethical and legal issues of genetic screening and testing, genetic counseling, confidentiality of genetic data, gene therapy, access to experimental treatment, population genetics, development of plant and animal biotechnologies, and neurosciences.<br />Second, the IBC opted for maximum transparency—by publicizing its findings, and by launching in 1995 an international consultation of over 200 international governmental and non-governmental organizations, eminent scholars and thinkers, and national institutions (such as academies of science and medicine, law faculties, ethics committees, and the like).<br />Ever since, the IBC has worked in synergy with all national ethics committees and associations involved in this particular field.<br />The Human Genome and Human Rights<br />As adopted by the UNESCO General Conference in 1997, the Universal Declaration on the Human Genome and Human Rights became the first universal legal instrument in the field of biology, medicine, and genetics.<br />Its chief merit lay in the balance it struck between guaranteeing respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms, and the need to ensure freedom of research.<br />In endorsing this Declaration in 1998, the United Nations General Assembly recognized the importance of formulating ethical principles regarding the application of science to human beings, and so acted well in advance of the explosion in human genetics and resulting debates and controversies.<br />The UNESCO Declaration brought key focus to bear on recognition of universal human dignity as the absolute moral imperative to preserve, in all research and applied technology regarding the human genome.<br />To quote the Declaration’s first article on Human Dignity and the Human Genome<br />the human genome underlies the fundamental unity of all members of the human family, as well as the recognition of their inherent dignity and diversity.<br />In a symbolic sense, it is the heritage of humanity.<br />Consonant with this concern for human dignity was the fourth article’s emphasis that "the human genome in its natural state shall not give rise to financial gains."<br />A World Commission for Ethics<br />COMEST<br />UNESCO’s establishment in 1998 of the World Commission on the Ethics of Scientific Knowledge and Technology (COMEST), chaired by H. E. Ms Vigdis Finnbogadottir, President of the Republic of Iceland from 1980 to 1996, was a further milestone in the elaboration of a co-ordinated global response to the critical challenges of our day.<br />The Commission’s mandate is vast, since it serves as an int