br />The logic behind this statement is simple<br />less meat consumption results in a decrease in feed grain demand and this leads to a reduction in water use for the production of these grains.<br />Although there is some truth in this statement, reality proves more complex.<br />As shown in Table 3, there is a huge difference in food and feed grain consumption between the different country groups.<br />Developed countries consume two to three times the amount of grains<br />sixty % of this is used as animal feed.<br />Table 3.<br />Per capita grain consumption<br />The PODIUM model was used to explore the impact of the consumption of animal products on water use in agriculture.<br />This "product" category includes red meat, white meat, eggs, milk, cheese, butter, and so on.<br />Because of data problems it is not possible to disaggregate feed consumption within this broad category.<br />The composition of this category varies between countries and contribute to differences in the amount of feed grains used per unit product (see below).<br />Fish products are not included in this analysis.<br />Figure 4 shows the results for India and the US.<br />Figure 4.<br />Animal product consumption and water demand in India and USA<br />In India, a reduction in meat consumption by 10% results in a reduction in need for primary water by four %.<br />In the United States, a reduction in the % age of meat in the diet by 10% reduces primary water requirements by 16%.<br />In both cases there is a decrease, but the difference in magnitude of decrease is striking.<br />The explanation lies in how animals are fed.<br />If animals rely on feed from irrigated land, as is common in the US, water requirements are highly sensitive to the % age of meat in the diet.<br />If on the other hand, animals graze, or eat crop residues, irrigation is less important in producing grains to feed animals.<br />A feed to meat conversion factor, defined as the amount of feed grain that is needed to produce one unit of animal products, captures this relation.<br />Based on data derived from FAOSTAT (UN Food and Agriculture Organization Statistical Databases), India used 0.06 kilograms of feed grains to produce 1000 kcal of animal products in 1995.<br />The US used more than 20 times as much for the same amount (1.3 kilograms per 1000 kcal).<br />The conversion factor not only varies considerably between country but will also be subject to changes in future decades.<br />The following factors contribute to differences and changes in the conversion factor<br />In some countries the use of feed grains is very low because farmers use grazing lands and crop waste to feed their cattle.<br />However, the carrying capacity of these traditional feed resources might reach its limit and additional production of animal products to meet future demands will most likely increasingly rely on the use of feed grains.<br />In countries where this is happening the conversion factor will increase.<br />In some countries there is a shift in consumption from red to white meats.<br />Since the feed requirements for white meats are lower, this trend will contribute to a decrease in conversion factor.<br />Because of technological improvements in the meat and dairy producing industry the "conversion efficiency" improves.<br />In other words, the conversion factor goes down with advancing technology.<br />In some countries feed grain consumption declines because of substitution for other feeds such as cassava (mainly grown under rain-fed conditions) and oil-meal.<br />Little is known about these factors and their interplay.<br />For example, the conversion ratio will most probably increase in India if there is a shift to more meat consumption-cattle will have eaten all crop residues and grazed most land and will need feed grains (the dotted line in Figure 4 depicts such a scenario).<br />In the US the conversion factor is likely to go down due to technological innovations and a shift from red to white meats.<br />A reduction in animal product consumption will most probably have a positive effect on moderating water demand in agriculture.<br />Nevertheless, it is quite uncertain as to what extent this reduction will help to mitigate water scarcity.<br />From a nutritional point of view the shift toward higher vegetable and fruit consumption is recommendable.<br />But it might result in a substantial increase in the demand for irrigation.<br />Vegetable production in most countries is not possible without irrigation and drainage systems.<br />But more importantly, the production of vegetables requires more water than cereals per calorie.<br />A case study from California indicates that it takes more than twice the amount of water to produce 1000 Kcal of vegetables than to produce the same amount of energy in cereals.<br />Irrigated Areas, Yields and Water Demands<br />With improved yields under irrigated conditions, the need for expansion of irrigated area decreases.<br />Less irrigated area means less need to develop additional water resources.<br />Large areas of irrigated land, mainly in developing countries, are performing under their potential.<br />Thus there is enormous scope to conserve water by increasing productivity in irrigated agriculture.<br />This hypothesis is clearly illustrated by the case of India. Average cereal yields under irrigated conditions in India amount to 2.7 tons per hectares, well below the world average of 3.3 tons per hectare.<br />Table 4 provides details on irrigated cereal yields obtained in other parts of the world.<br />Table 4<br />Cereal yields under irrigated and rain-fed conditions in selected countries<br />The PODIUM model was used to test the impact of increased productivity on water demand in agriculture.<br />Figure 5 depicts water requirements in India for different productivity scenarios.<br />If yields remain at 1995 levels and all additional production originates from expansion of the irrigated area, water requirements rise to more than 1200 HTM1.<br />The base case scenario predicts that yields will go up to 3.6 tons per hectare and total water requirements in irrigated agriculture rise to 900 HTM1.<br />If on the other hand yields increase to 4.6 tons per hectare, no additional irrigated area is required and hence no additional areas or infrastructure need to be developed and water requirements for agricultural purposes remain at 1995 levels.<br />Figure 5.<br />Relationship between yields and total diversions to agriculture for India under different scenarios<br />India will need an estimated additional 90 million tons of cereals to feed its population in 2025.<br />A study by Joshi (1996) quantified the yield gap by comparing actual and attainable yields obtained in demonstration plots managed by field research stations in different states across India. Figure 6 depicts the existing yield gap for the major states.<br />The study concluded that an additional 70 million tons of cereals could be obtained by bridging the observed yield gap.<br />This suggests that-theoretically-the bulk of additional cereal requirements in 2025 can be met by productivity increases in existing agricultural areas, without the need to develop new irrigated areas.<br />Figure 6.<br />Observed wheat yield gaps in Indian states<br />There is, however, evidence that in major irrigation schemes in India productivity per unit of land is stagnating since the first years of implementation, unlike schemes in the US where output values follow a rising trend in the long run.<br />Some researchers even claim that productivity in India declined over the last decade of the twentieth century, due to lack of proper investment in operation and maintenance<br />and declining soil fertility.<br />It is estimated that less than one fourth of the necessary operation and maintenance funds are allocated by the government and/or generated by water users through irrigation fees, resulting in deteriorating infrastructure and poor irrigation performance.<br />Although improvements in yields and water productivity are technically feasible, they may require substantial investments in infrastructure operation and maintenance, management, institutions, agro-services, and research.<br />These measures might not be easier to achieve or more cost-effective than expanding the irrigated area.<br />The policy options which India and many other developing countries face are between emphasis on the development of new water infrastructure and more irrigated land versus more productive use of existing irrigated agriculture.<br />In view of water scarcity the latter deserves more attention.<br />Another possibility is to increase food imports.<br />For many countries like India and China this is not a desirable option because of economic and political reasons.<br />In developing countries where rain-fed agriculture provides the major part of cereal production, increasing irrigated productivity will help but not solve the problem.<br />FOOD AND WATER DEMAND AND SUPPLY IN 2025<br />The irrigated area is so small that productivity gains in irrigated agriculture will be inconsequential.<br />FOOD AND WATER DEMAND AND SUPPLY IN 2025<br />In these areas it is imperative either to provide more water infrastructure, or to focus on rain-fed or small amounts of irrigation to supplement unpredictable rainfall.<br />Charlotte de Fraiture, David Molden, Upali Amarasinghe<br />Scope for Water Savings by Improved Efficiency<br />International Water Management Institute, Colombo, Sri Lanka<br />Since 80% of the world's developed water resources are diverted to irrigation, it is logical that improvements in irrigation would remove much stress on them.<br />Keywords<br />It is a common perception that irrigation wastes enormous quantities of water, especially from leaky canals and over-application of water.<br />Water use, water use projections, PODIUM model, diet, efficiency in irrigation, productivity in agriculture<br />This perception is in many cases not true, and the opportunity for real water savings is much less than thought.<br />Contents<br />The reason for this misperception lies in the incorrect use of the concept of "efficiency" used in irrigation.<br />Introduction<br />Generally, efficiency is defined by the amount of delivered water contributing to crop evaporation in a field or irrigation system.<br />The PODIUM Model<br />We refer to this definition as "classical efficiency".<br />What we can do to Change Future Water Use<br />The use of this concept at basin or country scale is misleading because it disregards the fact that water "losses" from inefficient irrigation systems are commonly recycled by other uses downstream.<br />Concluding Remarks<br />One person's loss is another person's supply.<br />Related Chapters<br />Instead of considering efficiency at irrigation system level, it is more correct to look at what happens at the larger scale of basins or countries.<br />Glossary<br />A useful concept in this context is "effective efficiency" taken at the basin or country scale.<br />Bibliography<br />This factor relates the total amount of crop evaporation contributed by water supplies (omitting rainfall contributions) to primary water supply at basin or country scale.<br />Summary<br />It differs from the classical definition in that it takes the primary water supply as the denominator instead of the total diversions.<br />Water use projections indicate that in the year 2025 humans will need more than 22% more primary water than in 1995, equivalent to 2820 HTM1 to meet all agricultural, domestic, and industrial needs.<br />Measured at different scales the effective efficiency tends to differ substantially.<br />In addition to using water more effectively, this projection indicates that mankind has to continue to place considerable financial resources into developing water resource infrastructure to store and divert water.<br />For example in the Bhakra irrigation scheme in northwestern India percolation from paddy fields replenish the shallow groundwater.<br />Water use is a function of population, diets, domestic, and industrial needs, and how effectively water resources are used.<br />This groundwater is pumped by numerous tube-wells located in the command area, which are used in conjunction with canal water.<br />When will human society start to reach the limits of its renewable water resources?<br />The classical efficiency of surface water may be low (35 to 45%) but in combination with tube-wells the effective efficiency in the sub-basin is close to 80%.<br />What is the potential for reducing future requirements by improvements in managing water resources or changing consumption patterns?<br />In Egypt, effective efficiency has been estimated at 70 to 80%.<br />A base case scenario generated by the PODIUM model, representing a likely scenario of water resource use, was used to explore these and related water supply and demand questions.<br />In tank irrigation prevalent in Sri Lanka and Tamil Nadu, drainage outflows from paddy fields upstream are captured in reservoirs located downstream.<br />Using PODIUM, it is estimated that in 1995, on a global average, a person requires 470 HTM1 of water per year.<br />Improvements in irrigation efficiency in irrigation schemes upstream will have direct negative impacts on water availability in systems downstream.<br />The base case scenario, using an assumed population growth of 33%, projects that by 2025 average annual water diversions amount to 450 HTM1.<br />Basically, there are two ways to increase the effective efficiency<br />Additional scenarios, using the PODIUM base case as a reference, explored sensitivity in water supply to population, diets, efficiency in irrigation, and productivity in irrigated and rain-fed agriculture.<br />increase classical efficiency at scheme level and increase recycling.<br />It was shown that changing to a less meat-intensive diet could save some water, but it depends heavily on in which countries this change takes place.<br />These are interrelated<br />Consuming more vegetables may offset gains made in consuming less meat.<br />as an irrigation scheme becomes more efficient, it "produces" less drainage water and hence the possibility of recycling downstream reduces.<br />There are gains to be made in water savings in agriculture, but these are far more limited in magnitude than commonly thought and do not go far beyond the base case scenario.<br />Improving classical efficiency at system level typically includes costly measures such as lining of canals, introducing alternative irrigation methods (e.g. drip or sprinkler), and management changes.<br />It may not be environmentally prudent to increase efficiency in water use beyond the gains already shown in the PODIUM base case.<br />Enhancing recycling may require check dams and pumps in drains or the construction of tube-wells to facilitate reuse of seepage water that replenishes shallow ground water.<br />Productivity gains in both irrigated and rain-fed agriculture certainly hold the most potential for reducing the need for additional water resources.<br />In some cases recycling may be cheaper and easier to achieve than improving efficiency at system level, but it carries the danger of environmental hazards such as build-up of salts and agro-chemicals.<br />But this will require significant changes in the way agriculture is carried out in developing countries.<br />Which measures-or combination of measures-are most appropriate differ between country and are dependent on physical and management characteristics of the basin.<br />Introduction<br />Theoretically, effective efficiency could reach close to 100%.<br />For millennia, humans have diverted water from its natural hydrologic course to meet basic human needs<br />However, it is desirable that some liquid water should flow out of the basin to flush out salts, meet environmental needs, and provide some buffer for dry years.<br />drinking, bathing, manufacturing, and food production.<br />For example, survival of coastal estuaries is dependent on some outflow of good quality water.<br />Except in the most arid areas, a major problem has been to develop more infrastructure for storing and diverting water to meet human needs.<br />The limit is set by the % age of primary water supply that is evaporated by different uses within the country or basin.<br />When will mankind start to reach the limits of its renewable water resources?<br />Generally, the % age should not exceed 70 to 80%.<br />How much more infrastructure must be developed to exploit more water resources?<br />The cut-off value of 70% is a rough estimate.<br />What is the potential for improvements in managing water resources?<br />Little is known about water quantities necessary for environmental purposes and this area merits further investigation.<br />Can humans change their habits to use a limited resource better?<br />In other words, a maximum of 70 to 80% of all primary water leaves the basin as vapor while a 20-30% allocation is made for outflow to environmental uses.<br />With more and more people facing water scarcity in cities and in agriculture, these are critical questions for the twenty-first century.<br />When this factor reaches around 100%, the results are falling water tables, buildup of salinity, and heavily polluted rivers.<br />To explore these questions, the International Water Management Institute (IWMI) developed a Policy Dialogue Model (PODIUM) to help global communities and nations understand key relationships between human water needs, water availability and water use.<br />Table 5.<br />The model was used extensively to facilitate understanding and promote discussions and consultations meetings leading to the 2000 World Water Forum.<br />Effective efficiency and reduction of primary water demand<br />This paper describes key results from the PODIUM modeling exercise.<br />Table 5 shows the results of the sensitivity analysis of effective efficiency on water demand and scope for improvement, using the PODIUM model.<br />It explores the relationship between global water use and key variables of population, diets, irrigated versus rain-fed agriculture, and productivity of water.<br />The scope for reducing primary water demand is limited to a maximum of 7.6% compared to 1995 conditions, an amount of 130 HTM1 of water.<br />In this way the effect of the most important water-related choices needing to be made in the near future can be understood.<br />The rather optimistic base case already allows for 6% of the increase, thus the saving potential over the base case is only one % or 18 HTM1.<br />The PODIUM Model<br />This analysis suggests that the scope for saving water by improving efficiency in irrigation is much less than commonly perceived.<br />Model Description<br />In irrigated areas suffering from waterlogging and rising-often saline-groundwater tables with limited scope for recycling, improving classical efficiency is essential to reduce environmental problems, rather than to save water.<br />The most important aspect of PODIUM is that it projects HTM1 what can happen HTM2 as opposed to HTM3 what will happen HTM4.<br />As far as possible, interventions that convert non-beneficial uses of water into beneficial uses should be pursued.<br />A wide variety of variables can be changed that have a major impact on water use and food production.<br />But these gains may not yield tremendous amounts of water to slow the need for growth in irrigation development.<br />It allows exploration of various key policy options that will have a major impact in this century.<br />The real place to find savings is in increasing the productivity of water consumed in agriculture, as discussed in the above section.<br />PODIUM is run on a national scale, and then results are compiled on a global scale to obtain a worldwide picture of water use and food production.<br />Rain-Fed versus Irrigated Agriculture<br />Computations are done in four steps (Figure 1).<br />One popular idea is to concentrate food production in rain-fed, rather than irrigated, areas to avoid adverse consequences of water infrastructure development.<br />The first estimates food grain requirements based on assumptions concerning population growth, calorie intake, and composition of diets.<br />At present more than three-quarters of the world's cultivated area is rain-fed, producing some 60% of the world's total grain production.<br />Second, food production is estimated based on expected yields and cultivated area under both irrigated and rain-fed conditions.<br />Because of its larger scale, productivity improvements in rain-fed agriculture will have a much larger impact on total production than in irrigated agriculture.<br />The projected production is then compared to the required production and country-wide surplus and deficit of food grains is estimated.<br />Furthermore, environmental problems often connected to irrigation development will be avoided and many poor farmers cultivating marginal lands are likely to benefit.<br />The third module estimates future water requirements by considering domestic, industrial, and agricultural needs.<br />An important question in this debate is to what extent rain-fed agriculture can contribute to the augmentation of food production.<br />Projected food production by irrigated agriculture is converted into water requirements.<br />Some fear that this will not be possible without a major increase in area under rain-fed agriculture.<br />Finally, food production is compared to demand, then future water requirements compared to availability of water, to obtain an indication of water scarcity.<br />Since productivity in rain-fed agriculture is less than in irrigated areas, an increase in production from rain-fed areas requires a greater area expansion than the same production increase from irrigated areas.<br />Figure 1.<br />Most of the suitable areas are already under cultivation, and hence expansion will be in marginal areas, characterized by erratic rainfall and low yields.<br />PODIUM Model Variables<br />Moreover, area expansion might only be possible at the expense of natural forests and wetlands.<br />It should be emphasized that reliable data on key factors in global water and food supply are lacking.<br />Others claim that there is still ample scope to improve the productivity of existing rain-fed areas.<br />Consequently, assessments of the water use situation and projections for the future involve a large amount of uncertainty.<br />They point to biotechnology as a possibility for developing drought-resistant varieties.<br />There are large gaps in knowledge about water resources, water use, and irrigated areas.<br />Or they emphasize the still largely unexplored possibilities for boosting yields by small scale supplementary irrigation and local water harvesting techniques.<br />Thus, there is an urgent need for more accurate data.<br />As input to this debate, the PODIUM model was used to answer the question<br />The figures used in the PODIUM model are based on published data sets, supplemented by "best estimates" by IWMI scientists and national collaborators working in the different countries.<br />What is the required increase in food grain production from rain-fed agriculture to meet all additional demands without increasing the irrigated area?<br />The Base Case Scenario<br />The base scenario is taken as the basis for total food requirements, irrigated yield growth, and overall cereal production level in 2025 for individual countries.<br />One of the scenarios that is formulated using the PODIUM model is the HTM1 IWMI Base Case HTM2 scenario.<br />Irrigated yields grow according to the trends observed during the last decade of the twentieth century, while the irrigated area remains at the 1995 level.<br />This scenario describes a future based on present trends, and a fairly optimistic view of the ability to adjust to problems and crises.<br />Figure 7 shows the results.<br />This base case will be used to focus the discussion in this article, and act as a baseline to compare sensitivity to various parameters.<br />Figure 7.<br />Within an overall framework of social, technical, and economic feasibility, it relies on substantial investments and changes in policies, institutions, and management systems to realize four major objectives<br />Present and future rain-fed production assuming zero-growth of irrigated area<br />Achieve an adequate level of per capita food consumption, partly through increased irrigation, to substantially reduce malnutrition and the most extreme forms of poverty.<br />The results indicate that under this scenario total rain-fed production needs to grow from 930 million tons in 1995 to 1238 million tons in 2025, an increase of 33%, compared to an increase of 3% as predicted in the base case scenario.<br />Provide sufficient water to the domestic and industrial sectors to meet basic needs and economic demands for water.<br />Total cultivated area in the majority of countries only slightly expanded over the last five years 1990-1995.<br />Increase food security and rural income in countries where a large % age of poor people depend on agriculture for their livelihoods through agricultural development and protection from excessive (and often highly subsidized) agricultural imports.<br />Assuming rain-fed areas remain constant over the subsequent 30 years, the rain-fed yield (world average) has to grow from 2.2 to 2.9 tons per hectares.<br />Introduce and enforce strong policies and programs to increase water quality and to support environmental uses of water.<br />This seems a modest and feasible increase (32%).<br />The base case scenario relies largely on existing trends in population growth, dietary changes, growth in agricultural area, and agricultural production.<br />But this world average masks the large differences between the country groups.<br />A relatively high growth in yield is assumed for countries which have low productivity levels, while in other countries growth in production is assumed to be relatively lower.<br />In developing countries with large irrigated areas, rain-fed yields have to increase from 1.7 to 3.1 tons per hectare (an increase of 82%).<br />Over-exploitation of water is limited to allow sufficient water for the environment.<br />It is unlikely these countries will be able to raise yields to the necessary extent, unless a major breakthrough in crop technology occurs.<br />Using these base case assumptions, PODIUM results indicate that<br />Despite many encouraging examples at a local scale, measures such as rainwater harvesting techniques and supplemental irrigation may not be sufficient to boost productivity up to the required level at national scale.<br />To meet domestic, industrial, and agricultural demands, a growth in primary water supplies of 22% is required.<br />Without increases in irrigated production, these countries will have to expand the rain-fed area by 65%-infeasible in most countries-or relax their policy of self-sufficiency and become importers.<br />Total diversions to these sectors increases from 3195 to 4125 HTM1 , a growth of 29%.<br />More likely, irrigated production in food supply in these countries will become more important.<br />One km3<br />Increasing irrigated productivity is the most effective way to reduce water needs.<br />liters.<br />In developing countries characterized by large rain-fed production, rain-fed yields need to increase from 1.5 to 2.2 ton per hectare (65%) to achieve self-sufficiency in cereals in 2025.<br />To give an idea of the order of magnitude, the High Aswan dam in Egypt, serving most of Egypt's agriculture, one of the biggest dams in the world, releases 55 HTM1 annually.<br />This might be feasible provided that more attention is 