iate measures are taken.<br />See Figure 3.<br />What is the scope for reducing the amount of water diverted to agriculture?<br />According to this scenario the irrigated area worldwide expands by 95 million hectares.<br />To curtail water demand in agriculture one could try to reduce food demand or increase food supply using the same amount or less water.<br />Primary diversions to agriculture increase by 17% compared to the 29% increase in area, due to improvements in water use in agriculture.<br />Key factors on the food demand side are population growth, consumption patterns, and international trade.<br />The global average of irrigated cereal yield is projected to increase from 3.3 to 4.7 tons per hectare.<br />The supply side is largely determined by productivity in irrigated and rain-fed areas and water use efficiency.<br />The base case scenario assumes that many high-income developing countries, such as Indonesia, India, and China, in their attempt for food self-sufficiency will increase agricultural production by expanding the area under cultivation with emphasis on irrigated agriculture.<br />Population growth has a direct bearing on water demands.<br />Low-income developing countries with rapid population growth will increasingly rely on food imports.<br />According to the base case scenario, every additional million inhabitants translate into a required expansion of 47 000 hectares of irrigated land and increase of 1230 liters of primary water supply per day to meet basic water needs.<br />Production from food exporting countries will be sufficient to meet the needs of increased cereal needs of food importers.<br />But increased per capita water use for domestic and industrial purposes as result of improving living standards in developing countries is likely to have a much greater impact on water requirements than population growth.<br />Figure 2.<br />A reduction in meat consumption will lead to a reduction in water demand, especially in developed countries where actual meat and feed grain consumption is high.<br />Sectoral Water Demand in 1995 and 2025<br />However due to changing meat-feed conversion ratios the extent of this impact remains uncertain.<br />The base case scenario concludes that despite improvements in productivity and efficiency, water scarcity at the national level will exist, a situation which will need to be managed<br />Globally, water is not a constraint in food production.<br />33% of the population of the 45 studied countries will live in HTM1 physical water scarce HTM2 areas.<br />Food produced in water abundant places could be exported to water short countries (also coined as "trade in virtual water").<br />Water limits these countries' ability to produce enough food after industrial and domestic consumption requirements are met.<br />For many developing countries striving for food self-sufficiency (like India, China, and Indonesia) economic and political objectives are important, thus trade does not play as large a role.<br />45% of the population will live in countries with substantially underdeveloped water resources, requiring development of 25% or more additional water supplies, in the form of small or large scale facilities.<br />The main scope for reducing water requirements lies in the improvement of land and water productivity in irrigated agriculture ("more crop per drop").<br />The situation in these countries is termed HTM1 economic water scarcity HTM2 because of the need for substantial investments in financial resources to meet future needs.<br />To reach the yield increase assumptions in the base case scenario will require major efforts in water management.<br />22% of the population will live in countries, mainly developed countries, with little or no water scarcity.<br />However, there remains scope in developing countries to substantially raise yields.<br />There is enough water available to meet basic human and environmental needs.<br />Improving performance and incre