e figures should be explained.<br />Fresh water makes up the other three percent or 37 million HTM1.<br />Some of this water is withdrawn (taken for storage or use) but not actually used.<br />Of this residual, 75 % is locked up in glaciers and icebergs.<br />Instead, some of it is returned directly to the source after use.<br />Aquifers store another 8 million HTM1 of fresh water.<br />In other cases, water is reused and recirculated, often for industrial purposes.<br />Only 200 000 HTM1 of the 1 360 million HTM2 can be found in rivers, lakes and wetlands.<br />The term 'gross water use', is used to describe the total amount of water used, even if some of it has been reused several times.<br />We can therefore see that, although water is a common element to be found in every sector of the biosphere, that only part of that reserve can be used to keep humans alive and our complex socio-economic societies from going.<br />Another useful term is 'water consumption' which refers to water that has been used in such a way that it cannot be immediately reused.<br />What should also be kept in mind is that this fresh water needs storing, transportation and purification before it can be used by humans.<br />Evaporation and contamination are two ways that water is 'consumed'.<br />This voluminous amount of water, as part of a number of interdependent ecosystems help to support life on the living planet.<br />Once it has evaporated, it has to fall as rain before it re-enters the supply of freshwater from which the withdrawals take place.<br />These systems range from fresh water river ecosystems to marine environments.<br />Another interesting point about global water use figures is that they are substantially lower than predicted.<br />The ocean waters are not wasted, as is commonly believed, but it is a necessary reservoir of the hydrologic cycle.<br />A couple of decades ago, it was thought that water use in 2000 would be around the 7 000 HTM1 yr mark.<br />The hydrologic cycle can broadly be defined as the world-wide flow of water.<br />That prediction, like so many others, has been proved incorrect because of a new approach to water by some of the biggest users<br />Water evaporates from the oceans to the atmosphere, from the atmosphere to the oceans and land by precipitation, from the land to the oceans by run-off from streams and rivers and subsurface groundwater flow, and from the land to the atmosphere by evaporation.<br />This constant flow of water is powered by the heat of the sun and the earth's gravitational pull.<br />It is also within the hydrologic cycle that water change face from solid to liquid and from liquid to vapour.<br />As has been discussed above, the terrestrial fresh water in rivers, lakes and wetlands represents a very small fraction of the water in the hydrologic cycle, but it still is of utmost importance in providing our water resources.<br />Humans are therefore an integral part of the hydrologic cycle, although we do not, as biological organisms produce any water like plants do, we are still users of some of the water in the biosphere.<br />Because water is abundant at the global level, there is a false sense of security, which is compounded by the fact that water is not available in the same amount on all continents and regions across the world.<br />Human development can be seen as part of the hydrologic cycle.<br />Our quality of life (at least in some parts of the world) would not have been possible if it were not for interventions in the hydrologic cycle.<br />Because rivers form part of this cycle, human interference in rivers have an influence on part of the circulation of water in the biosphere.<br />In other words, our water related activities have an impact on the quantity and quality (discussed in more detail below) of water resources.<br />From a political science perspective, terms like state sovereignty and government control can only be applied to a certain section of the entire hydrologic cycle.<br />The reason for this is that water falls under government control and state sovereignty only while it is on the territory of a given state entity, which is furthermore a temporary phase in the development of that water resource.<br />In spite of that, a country can still utilise the water under its control even if it is not on its territory anymore.<br />GLOBAL AND REGIONAL FRESHWATER RESOURCES<br />Examples of these non-territorial uses of water would be the desalinisation of sea water and, in theory, cloud seeding.<br />GLOBAL AND REGIONAL FRESHWATER RESOURCES<br />Nonetheless, the momentary condition of terrestrial water can be explained by looking at the behaviour of water after it had precipitated as rain, hail or snow over state territories.<br />R. Meissner<br />It will<br />Research Associate at the African Water Issues Research Unit at the University of Pretoria, South Africa<br />After a certain duration either flow out of the territory into a river that is under the sovereign and governmental control of a given state<br />Keywords<br />Be absorbed into an aquifer which can also be under sovereign and governmental control<br />water paradigm, virtual water, blue water, green water, freshwater resources, global, regional, distribution, future, water needs<br />Evaporate back into the atmosphere or<br />Contents<br />Be collected into a lake, marsh or wetland that is also under the same control.<br />Introduction<br />In order for humans to utilise this small amount of water it is necessary to build water supply projects to capture the water, before it flows into the ocean.<br />A Change in the Perception of Water<br />After such a scheme has been constructed there will be no reassurance that the water will remain in a fixed position.<br />Perspectives of Global and Regional Freshwater Resources<br />It can still percolate into the ground or be lost due to evaporation by the sun's heat.<br />Freshwater Quantity, Quality and Distribution Across the Globe and in Regions<br />These water supply schemes can take a number of shapes.<br />Global Water Needs for the Future<br />It will be either a large dam, to produce hydro-electricity (the Ruacana Dam on the Kunene River shared by Namibia and Angola), or to supply agriculture, urban and industrial areas with water (the Calueque Scheme in Angola on the Kunene River).<br />Conclusion<br />Alternatively it can involve the construction of a network of large dams with related infrastructure to transfer water from one basin to another where the water is in short supply - the Lesotho Highlands Water Project (LHWP).<br />Related Chapters<br />It can furthermore be in the form of a pipeline, with pump stations to transport the water against the pull of gravity to centra with the greatest demand - the proposed Okavango Pipeline in Namibia is an example.<br />Glossary<br />Such a project can also involve an aquifer abstraction network, utilising groundwater resources (Libya's man-made river, Namibia's Karstveld groundwater supply project, and Israel's schemes that pump water from the Westbank are examples).<br />Bibliography<br />In this respect, humans are an integral part of the hydrologic cycle, intervening in it and moving water, so to speak, against its will to where it is needed.<br />Biographical Sketch<br />It is in these cases that the quantity and distribution of water resources come to play an important role in socio-economic development.<br />Summary<br />It is argued that the current global water use is about 4 500 HTM1.<br />The management of global and regional freshwater resources has changed significantly over the past few decades, in many countries of the developed North and developing South.<br />Notwithstanding, the portion of available fresh water seems to be adequate to supply water to a growing demand.<br />This is exemplified by the changing water paradigm that brought about a change in our perception of water resources and the life support systems