mpact on future global and regional freshwater management.<br />For instance, although India has adequate average water availability of 2 500 HTM1 per capita per year, the federal state of Rajasthan has a yearly access of only 550 HTM2 per capita.<br />Yet, not all regions and states will use water in the future in the same manner.<br />With respect to water distribution across seasons, Bangladesh suffers annually from monsoon flooding which is followed by severe dry seasons with water shortages occurring during such periods.<br />The two variables will have different effects on global and regional freshwater utilisation.<br />Water is not only irregularly distributed over time and space it is also a reality regarding different socio-economic groups within a society.<br />Introduction<br />In general, rural households have less access to water than urban families, especially in the developing world.<br />Sustainable economic development hinges on the availability of water resources, especially in the developing world.<br />For instance, in Botswana the percentage of the rural population with access to safe water is about 53 %, while 100 % of the urban population has access to safe water.<br />Where the industrial sector is not well developed, or weak, water consumption in it can be as high as 80 %.<br />In Angola only 15 % of the rural population has access to safe water, while 69 % of the urban population gets fresh water supplies.<br />This offers support to the socio-political importance of water in the economic development of many regions around the world.<br />In Lesotho there is an inverse situation of access to safe water between the rural and urban population.<br />The importance of water to these regions is reflected in the vision of the water sector within some regions.<br />About 64 % of the rural population has access, while only 14 % of the urban population has available water supplies.<br />For instance, the water vision of the Southern African Development Community (SADC) states that SADC attempts to "attain the sustainable, integrated planning, development, utilisation and management of water resources that contribute to the attainment of SADC's overall objective of an integrated regional economy on the basis of balance, equity and mutual benefit for all member states "..<br />The case of Lesotho, at least in the developing world, is, however, the exception and not the rule.<br />Within this vision water is directly linked to the economic development of all SADC member states, which is a good indication of the socio-economic and political importance of water.<br />Water is therefore not only ill distributed between geographical regions and climatological seasons, but also between different socio-economic sectors within states.<br />In other parts of the developing south, it is estimated that more than 1.7 billion people, spread over 80 countries, are suffering from water shortages which further signify the importance of water to humans and the societies they live in.<br />It is for this reason that states implement water supply projects and water related policies, in order to make water available to as large a portion of society as possible and to stimulate socio-economic development.<br />Basic human consumption, survival and economic well-being of a large part of the population residing within developing and developed regions rests on this scarce resource.<br />Water scarcities can become a problem when there is a limited volume of water available in proportion with the size of the population and the level of socio-economic development.<br />This suggests that there should be enough water to cater to the needs of society and that the water resources should also be of a good quality.<br />A state that has 1 000 to 1 600 HTM1 per capita per year of water availability can be categorised as water stressed.<br />As one of the most important resources available to all life support systems, and constituting such a system itself, water resources are becoming more and more exploited while the quantity and quality thereof is becoming inextricably limited.<br />Major problems with respect to the supply of water can be experienced by such states in times of drought.<br />This is due to the fact that a number of constraints and limiting factors, such as population growth, industrialization and urbanization, is taxing global and regional water resources to their limits.<br />Where water availability is less than 1 000 HTM1 per person per year, states are classified as water scarce.<br />If this sounds very pessimistic, there is however room for optimism.<br />When water availability falls below this level, due to a number of factors, the scarcity of water can be a crippling limitation on socio-economic development, environmental quality and the distribution of people.<br />This idealism lies in the fact that there are different perspectives regarding the global water balance and regional water resources.<br />People might migrate to other more water rich regions in order to escape the debilitating effects of such dire water scarcities.<br />These perspectives can offer practical solutions to our water problems and can become one of the major practices of future generations to develop and use the water in the hydrologic cycle in a more sustainable manner.<br />Within the next 25 years water shortages will start showing a dramatic increase in a number of regions and states across the entire world.<br />In this article the different perspectives of the global water balance and regional water resources will be discussed.<br />By 2025 it is estimated that between 46 to 52 states, out of a total of about 190, with an estimated combined population of about three billion people will reach the stage of water stress.<br />Any discussion of future water needs must acknowledge that while it is possible to sketch a picture of the global future, water use is a local activity that affects individuals and small communities as much as it does sovereign states and large commercial enterprises.<br />This will mean that this large population will have between 1 000 and 1 600 HTM1 per capita of water to support their basic needs and economic activities.<br />Global averages are only useful when they allow a better understanding of individual and community use of water.<br />What is also of importance in this equation is the quality of these water resources, in that if water is highly polluted, it can contribute to water scarcities due to a drop in freshwater quantities.<br />That water is unevenly available is a principle that will guide this analysis of future water needs.<br />In the next section we will take a closer look at the quality of the planet's water resources and the source which can degrade the quality.<br />One community may have more water than it can use while another may have so little water that its survival is at stake.<br />The Quality of Global and Regional Freshwater Resources<br />While humans and ecosystems can tolerate and sometimes depend on an uneven supply of water, there is generally a continuous and growing demand for water.<br />One of the most pressing problems concerning the world's freshwater resources is the pollution thereof.<br />It can be said that the flow of water through the biosphere is like the flow of blood through a body.<br />The pollution of freshwater resources can be caused by a number of anthropogenic activities that have a debilitating effect on the quality of this crucial resource.<br />The relationship between the constant demand for water and the uneven supply is crucial.<br />The most notable sources of freshwater quality degradation are<br />If well-managed, it can help society in its search for a better life.<br />Industrialization, which causes ever increasing levels of effluents being discharged into freshwater resources.<br />If water needs are not carefully matched to the supply, disaster becomes unavoidable.<br />Inadequately treated or untreated urban, industrial and agricultural sewage.<br />Need and demand must conform to supply and access.<br />Chemicals being released into freshwater sources from irrigated agricultural areas.<br />In this topic some of these issues will be discussed in more detail.<br />Wastes from mining activities.<br />The topic starts of with a look at the changing water paradigm, which has had, and is still having a large impact on the way humans perceive water and how it is utilised.<br />Thermal pollution from coal fired and nuclear power plants.<br />Two perspective of global and regional freshwater resources will be discussed in the second part of the topic<br />Not only is there a number of pollution sources of freshwater, but also a large variety of contaminants.<br />virtual water and blue and green water.<br />These include, among others<br />These perspectives are part of the changing water paradigm.<br />Cleaners, like soap and solvents, found in industries and households with which we wash ourselves, our clothes, motor vehicles, industrial commodities, etc.<br />In the third section of the topic global freshwater quantity, quality and distribution will come under the magnifying glass.<br />Pesticides, used mainly in agriculture but also for domestic gardening activities.<br />In the last part, the future needs of the earth's population for freshwater will be discussed.<br />Discarded petroleum products, like oil and petrol (gas).<br />A Change in the Perception of Water<br />Heavy metals, like lead, zinc, mercury and a host of others.<br />From the onset it is important to note that water scarcities are a local, or river basin, phenomenon.<br />Fertilizers and other plant nutrients used widely in agriculture and our gardens.<br />The importance of this is quite extreme regarding strategic decision-making, for it allows for a conceptual difference to be developed between water scarcity at a local level, and the search for a solution at the regional or global level.<br />Oxygen exhausting elements, for instance, wastes from food processing plants, abattoirs and paper and pulp factories.<br />Although there is enough water on earth, the distribution thereof is of such a nature that it is in abundance in some places and in others it can be extremely scarce.<br />Agents causing illnesses like cholera, hepatitis, typhoid fever and dysentery.<br />Water is therefore both scarce and bountiful over space.<br />These source and elements of freshwater pollution can cause severe damage to aquatic life support systems.<br />Not only that, it can also be limited or ample over time.<br />One of the most severe cases of the collapse of such a system can be found in Central Asia - the Aral Sea.<br />Some regions of the world might experience periods of adequate rainfall while at others droughts can persist for some time.<br />The Aral Sea is today one of the worst environmental disasters which was caused by irrigation and the subsequent release of agricultural chemicals.<br />For instance, due to the El Ni?±o/Southern Oscillation (ENSO) effect Southern Africa have seen over the past 20 years periods of drought (1981-1985 and 1990-1995) with intervals of sufficient rainfall that provided for most of the water requirements for the economic activities of the region.<br />The Aral Sea is a unique ecosystem which is fed by two rivers without one flowing out of it.<br />During such periods of drought many rivers will not be able to supply water to large urban, industrial and agricultural communities.<br />The water balance of the Aral Sea is kept in equilibrium with the high evaporation rate of the in flowing water from the Amudar'ya and Syrdar'ya Rivers.<br />In fact, during the severe drought of the 1930s in South Africa many of the large and important rivers, like the Orange and Vaal Rivers, stopped flowing.<br />In the early 1970s the Soviet government implemented a scheme to divert the two rivers to irrigate over 18 million hectares of cotton and rice.<br />Rivers are therefore an important part of the hydrologic cycle and an important source of water for humans.<br />This caused the Aral Sea to shrink in size by three quarters of its original size.<br />A fundamental characteristic of traditional water resources planning, is that it has regularly and persistently concluded that future water demands will exceed actual water supplies, mainly due to the unchecked advancement of the three drivers.<br />What resulted was that most of the fish species in the Sea became extinct and the fishing industry collapsed.<br />This will be called supply sided management (SSM).<br />Yet, from a water quality perspective the disaster was also severe.<br />In terms of current social science theory, this form of management tends to focus on what has been defined as a first order resource scarcity - i.e. the scarcity of a natural resource such as water.<br />The salinity level of the Aral Sea's waters tripled and salt-dust storms swept across the area of the exposed sea bed.<br />Supply Sided Management was the dominant mode of water development and management in the United States of America (USA) prior to the 1980's, with the Bureau of Reclamation (BoR) and Army Corps of Engineers (ACoE) being the main instruments.<br />Due to the falling water table the sewage system of many of the communities living around the Aral Sea also failed and typhoid rates rose thirty-fold.<br />Significantly, these organizations spawned a global paradigm due to the fact that they trained many of the hydraulic engineers who subsequently went on to construct the world's largest dams.<br />Subsequently nine out of ten children were diagnosed as being permanently ill.<br />The main concern wit respect to change in thinking about water resources development is that of sustainable water use.<br />In the early 1990s there was even an outbreak of bubonic plague.<br />The scale of water mobilization has thus altered