ed or partially treated waste water is used for irrigation purposes underground and surface water resources can be contaminated with water borne diseases, with dire effects on people using these freshwater resources for their daily water allowance.<br />There has been strong opposition between and inside organizations responsible for water resources management.<br />Effluents discharged from factories and households end up in rivers and streams which have severe impacts for people and communities living downstream of the source of pollution.<br />For instance, during the 1990s the Namibian government started with plans to pipe water from the Okavango River to supply water to its urban and industrial centres in the central regions of the country.<br />This suggests that the degradation of the water quality of some areas are not confined to that locality.<br />These plans were feverishly opposed by environmental interest groups, concerned about the ecological integrity of the Okavango Delta.<br />When states in the upper reaches of an international river basin pollute the water available to downstream riparians, international friction between sovereign states can be a consequence.<br />These non-state entities even came up with alternative plans to augment Namibia's water supply.<br />An example from the Danube River system in Eastern Europe will suffice to illustrate this phenomenon.<br />One of these plans was the implementation of water demand management (WDM) across the entire country.<br />Pollution of water resources and the subsequent lowering of the water quality is therefore not only a local phenomenon which has an effect on people's access to safe water.<br />Yet, the strategies of the interest groups were countered by the Namibian government, invoking the sovereign right of a state to develop its water resources as it sees fit.<br />Across the entire planet many people do not have access to safe drinking water.<br />Furthermore the methods mentioned are not