ous kinds of soluble material as molecules or ions at a wide range of concentrations.<br />Theoretically, pure water consists only of water molecules ( HTM1 ) composed of one oxygen atom (O) and two hydrogen atoms (H), which are slightly dissociated to hydrogen ions ( HTM2 ) and hydroxyl ions ( HTM3 ), and containing no impurity.<br />In more precise terms, water forms cluster of a few to tens of water molecules which are aggregated to each other by hydrogen bonding.<br />Actually, water dissolves and suspends various kinds of impurities.<br />Ultra-pure wateris the result of currently available technologies which remove the impurities as much as possible to approach theoretical pure HTM1.<br />Ultra-pure water and pure water are different in their degrees of purity.<br />Pure water simply means water whose ion components are removed<br />therefore, it is also called de-ionized water (DI water).<br />On the other hand, ultra-pure water removes impurities such as suspended solid components, dissolved gas components, and microbes, to the greatest extent possible with state-of-art technologies.<br />Therefore, ultra-pure water cannot be defined chemically but it is a general term that has a certain range of the quality.<br />In other words, ultra-pure water is water with the highest purity currently achievable.<br />Integration level of LSI and trend of quality of ultra-pure water<br />LSI manufacturing processes require ultra-pure water with very high quality for cleaning, in order to avoid contamination of silicon wafers or computer chips.<br />The demand of more integrated LSIs leads to a requirement for higher quality of water.<br />As shown in Table 4, generation by generation, the degree of integration quadrupled and the pattern size halved through technological development.<br />The smaller became the pattern sizes of LSI, the higher was the quality of water required.<br />This iteration produced the current achievement of very high quality of ultra-pure water that is nearly theoretical pure HTM1 with very low fine particles, dissolved gases, and microbes, as well as ions.<br />Table 4.<br />Generations of LSI and required quality of water<br />Recent ultra-pure water<br />Very advanced systems for processing highly pure water involve high installation cost as well as maintenance cost.<br />Recently, the wet cleaning process has been re-evaluated HTM1 including the cleaning methods.<br />The balance between the water quality and the cost of achieving the acceptable performance is being considered.<br />There is a trade-off between running cost in processing ultra-pure water and producing the highest quality of water for all purposes.<br />Processing method of ultra-pure water<br />In order to produce ultra-pure water, source water is firstly extracted from various sources, such as wells, rivers, lakes, etc.<br />A wide variety of impurities can be found in such water.<br />Definitely, the choice of water source is of primary importance since the level of impurities greatly affects the operation load of the water treatment plant.<br />In many cases, well water or river water is used due to the geographical conditions of semiconductor plants.<br />Clearly the source water with the lowest impurity level will be chosen.<br />As shown in Figure 3, ultra-pure water processing is subdivided into three systems, i.e. the pre-processing system for removing suspended particles that are mostly solid matter, the primary pure water processing system for removing dissolved gases, and the secondary pure water manufacturing system (usually called subsystem) for producing highly pure water (ultra-pure water) and maintaining the purity.<br />Table 5 shows typical treatment technologies to treat particular impurities.<br />Figure 3.<br />Ultra-pure water processing system<br />Table 5.<br />Typical treatment technologies and removable impurities<br />Pre-processing system<br />The pre-processing system removes a significant part of the suspen