er of ecosystem services supplied by both blue water and green water.<br />Furthermore the methods mentioned are not yet globally accepted practices and they may not be a permanent solution to our water problems.<br />Blue and green water are therefore two significant water resources on which socio-economic development rests.<br />Notwithstanding these barriers, the new water paradigm is here to remain in addressing unresolved water problems.<br />Sustainable development of human societies will, in light of the changing water paradigm, be quite an important issue when it comes to the exploitation of these water resources.<br />States have already started with the implementation of some of these measures and interest groups are already advocating the implementation of some of these actions such as in the case of Namibia.<br />Conclusion<br />For instance, WDM has been initiated by a number of states in the Southern African region.<br />The changing water paradigm ushered in a new era in the management of the planets water resources.<br />Most notable of these are Botswana and South Africa.<br />These changes have been driven by a number of elements like the change in water demand in a number of states, the role and involvement of environmental interest groups highlighting the destructive aspects of WRMPs and the increasing cost of WRMPs.<br />In South Africa the working for water programme (WWP) is but one of these actions with respect to the change in thinking about water resources management.<br />Prior to the changing water paradigm, water resources and the management thereof where in the traditional sense with respect to supplying more water to a growing world population, accompanied in reased urbanization and industrialization.<br />The WWP is an initiative built on the approach of the eradication of alien plant species within individual river basins.<br />Virtual water represents an alternative to the traditional perspective of the global water balance and regional water resources, in that it links water resources (raw water used for the production of commodities and services) with that of interdependent international trade on a global scale.<br />These plants use a lot of water and by getting rid of them we can improve the run-off of rivers and streams.<br />By trading in virtual water items, the dire threat of water shortages on a regional and river basin level can be addressed in a meaningful manner, by way of reaching into the global virtual water resource pool.<br />In Botswana the Water Master Plan (WMP) proposes that WDM should be initiated alongside expanding water supply sources.<br />Hampering such a strategy could be the protectionist trade barriers that are erected by states in order to facilitate a growing national economy.<br />This is a departure from earlier approaches that concentrated on water supply management (WSM).<br />These barriers can bring about a new sort of scarcity in global and regional water resources, which can be called structural imposed virtual water resource scarcities (SIVWRS).<br />These changes in the thinking about water resources management represents a change in the paradigm of human water use.<br />Such a situation will be characterised by the fact that virtual water commodities are abundant on a global scale, but scarce within the structures of the IPE due to trade protectionist policies imposed by states to protect jobs on the national economic front.<br />Many environmental interest groups have adopted the promotion of such measures as a central feature of their policy and influencing endeavours, in order to bring about more sustainable water planning efforts on the part of governments around the world.<br />Blue and green water, on the other hand, are also alternative perspectives of water resources in that it looks at the hydrologic cycle from a different conceptual lens.<br />Currently the World Conservation Union (IUCN) in Southern Africa has, over the past two years, been