s is understood as the minimum time interval from the beginning of the project realization, after which the integral effect becomes positive.<br />The discount rate HTM1 established for the year HTM2 is used for reducing expenditures, results and effects of different time periods to the value of this parameters at the initial period.<br />It is equal to the rate of income for the capital acceptable for investor.<br />This value is connected with the discount coefficient HTM1 by the expression<br />In addition to the traditional expenditures for purification, the compulsory payments established by the legislation norms are included into the production cost.<br />Net discount income (NDI) is determined as the sum of current effects at the entire calculation period reduced to the initial step or as the surplus of the integrated income characteristics over integral expenditures<br />where HTM1 is the sum of expenditures at the step t, HTM2 is the result on steps (income from water supply and other income)<br />HTM1 is the sum of investments at the step t<br />HTM1 is the sum of discounted investments, i.e.<br />When the NDI of the considered project version is positive, then this version is efficient at the particular discount rate.<br />The question of its acceptance is considered only in such a case.<br />The more is NDI, the more efficient is the project version.<br />The losses are included into the expression (5) with the sign &#8220;plus&#8221;, and the income with the sign &#8220;minus&#8221;.<br />NDI expresses the difference between the sum of the reduced effects and the value of investments reduced to the same moment of time ( HTM1 ).<br />The income index (IncInd) is the ration of the sum of the reduced effects and the sum of investments<br />This parameter is closely connected with NDI<br />if the NDI is positive, the IncInd &gt; 1.<br />The flow and balance of real money are calculated for the financial substantiation of the technologies.<br />Three forms of activity are differentiated, when the technological project is brought in existence<br />investing, operational, and financial.<br />The flow of real money is the difference between the intake and outtake of finances earned from investing and operational activity in every year of project realization (on the every step of calculation).<br />The flow of real money from investing activity includes the forms of income from sale of the actives and expenditures for acquiring ground, buildings, constructions, and equipment distributing along the periods (steps) of the calculation.<br />In essence, it includes the investments along the years of bringing the project in existence.<br />The flow of the real money from operational activity includes income from sale of water to the customers, exploitation expenditures, depreciation expenses, and taxes.<br />The flow of the financial activity includes all sorts of credits (intake) and redemption of all sorts of credit loans (outflow).<br />If the balance of real money becomes negative in some year (at the some step) that means that the project could be brought in existence in this particular form.<br />It is necessary in this case to increase the income part of the project and to decrease its expenditure part.<br />The project cannot be brought in existence in the proposed form even despite its high efficiency characteristics, if at the first year of the productive stage the balance of the real money flow would become negative.<br />It is necessary in this case to alter the project by increasing the income part of it and/or decreasing its expenditure part.<br />Also, the additional sources of founding could be found.<br />Thereafter, it is necessary to repeat once more the calculations.<br />Optimization of the complex of technological processes of water purification<br />The choice of optimum parameters of controlling the technological processes of natural and wastewater purification is very important in the practice of water 